  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nAs high pressure builds over the Midwest and into the Great Lakes, a \nstretch of several days of cooler than normal temperatures and \ncomfortable humidity starts today! This surface high pressure will \nallow for northerly winds to usher in cooler air; highs are only \nexpected to be in the mid 70s with lows dropping into the low to mid \n50s! This pattern change will feel refreshing and is a welcome \nreprieve from the heat and humidity earlier this month. Lows in the \n50s, while unusual from what we have been experiencing, are not that \nuncommon in July. We typically average about 5 nights with lows \nbelow 60 degrees in July. The coolest night is expected tonight into \nThursday morning, with widespread lows in the mid 50s. It is \npossible that some of our rural, low lying areas get to 50 or below. \nNBM probabilities currently have about a 15% chance of this tonight, \nbut chances are less than 5% for Fort Wayne and South Bend, where \nlows will be a touch higher, likely closer to 52-53 degrees. Record \nlows are safe this week as many of them are in the mid 40s. While \nnot record breaking, we will certainly welcome these cooler, crisp \nnights with lower humidity! \n\nAlthough the weather is quiet, the cold front that came through \nyesterday has stirred up southern Lake Michigan. A Beach Hazards \nStatement and corresponding High Swim Risk will be in effect for La \nPorte County, IN and Berrien County, MI today and tonight. Northerly \nwind gusts to 25 kts, waves of 5 to 9 feet, and strong currents will \ncreate life-threatening conditions for swimmers and boaters. Strong \nrip, structural, and longshore currents are expected today. Strong \nlongshore and structural currents will be especially present on the \nnorth side of piers. Stay out of the water and off of piers! \nConditions will be much calmer on Thursday and into the weekend with \nlow swim risks each day.\n\nGradually warmer into the weekend, with highs in the mid to upper \n80s by Sunday. Moisture increases too, which means humidity likely \ngoes up as the weekend progresses. A frontal system brings the next \nchances for rain/storms Sunday into Monday, although confidence is \nuncertain on exact timing. Our forecast area will be on the \nperiphery of a large upper level ridge to our west, so if we can get \nsufficient instability and moisture, we may be able to get \nstorms that ride the edge of the ridge. Severe weather is not \nout of the question but with high spread in model guidance, \nconfidence is very low for Sunday/Monday at this time.",
